          %h1#section_table_of_contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#the_problem"} The Problem
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_entries"} Related entries
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            For any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %mi n
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str n Cat
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+n-categories"} strict n-categories
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are strict
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^C
            are strict
            = succeed "-functors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to D
            %em strict
            = succeed "-natural" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em strict
            higher
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transfors"} transfors
          %p
            On the other hand, one expects the weak
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n Cat
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+n-categories"} weak n-categories
            to be cartesian closed in the
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ×
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi n
                  %mi Cat
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  n Cat(C\times D, E)
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  n Cat\big(C, Hom(D,E)\big)
                  \\mathrlap{\,.}
          %p
            In this case,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(D,E)
            should consist of weak functors, pseudo transformations (which are natural up to coherent
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ×
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\times D
            (probably) means the same thing in the strict and weak worlds,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi D
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D^C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo ,
                  %mi E
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(D,E)
            are not the same, and in general not even
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray+tensor+products"} Gray tensor products
            are part of the general philosophy of trying to understand weak higher structures without getting bogged down in the theory of weaker higher-er structures. One instance of this philosophy is the use of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            (or more general types of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n=2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+object"} cofibrant
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            and can be regarded as a definition of “weak functor” for large values of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/folk+model+structure"} folk model structure
            for 2-categories and
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://www.dpmms.cam.ac.uk/~rhgg2/Womaps/Womaps.html"} this paper
          %p
            Gray tensor products attempt to extend this to deal with weak
            = succeed "-transfors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\gt 0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal
            and/or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"} closed
            structure. This began with John Gray, who showed that the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Str
                  %mn 2
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Str2Cat
            is a non-symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
            in which one internal-hom
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Lax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lax(C,D)
            consists of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-functors"} strict 2-functors
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+natural+transformations"} lax natural transformations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modifications"} modifications
            (the other contains oplax transformations instead). A straightforward modification produces a closed symmetric monoidal structure based on
            %em pseudo
            natural transformations; the monoidal product is now called the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray+tensor+product"} Gray tensor product
          %p
            Later various authors constructed analogous tensor products for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-categories"} strict ∞-categories
            and strict ω-groupoids, here called the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Crans-Gray+tensor+product"} Crans-Gray tensor product
            = succeed "-functors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "-transfors" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\gt 0
          %h2#the_problem The Problem
          %p
            The (pseudo version of the) Gray tensor product of 2-categories is used to define
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray-categories"} Gray-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semistrict+n-category"} semistrict
            3-categories. This is very convenient because Gray-categories are just
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} categories enriched
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Str
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi Gray
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Str2Cat,\otimes_{Gray})
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p However, there is a problem:
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                No category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SS
                      %mi n
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SS n Cat
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+definition+of+higher+category"} algebraic
                = succeed "-categories" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interchange+law"} interchange
                and strict functors can admit a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"} biclosed structure
                that contains non-strict transformations.
          %p
            Now there are lots of adjectives in this statement; for now just notice that the progression “categories, 2-categories, Gray-categories, Gray-cat-categories?”
            %em is
            producing categories of algebraic
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            To see why this no-go statement holds, imagine some hypothetical algebraic notion of semistrict
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            be semistrict
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F,G:C\to D
            (strict) functors; then a transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha:F\to G
            should probably consist of
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                components
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mi x
                        %mo :
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo →
                        %mi G
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_x: F(x) \to G(x)
            %li
              %p
                equivalences
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi x
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi y
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_f: G(f) \circ \alpha_x \simeq \alpha_y F(f)
                for
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo :
                        %mi x
                        %mo →
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:x\to y
            %li
              %p and higher data,
            %li
              %p satisfying axioms.
          %p
            Now the question is, how is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_{g f}
            related to the composite of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_g
            and
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_f
            = succeed "-categories," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "." do
              %em equal
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em must
            be equal.
          %p
            Consider the following: a transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⇒
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\Rightarrow G: C\to D
            should be the same as a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \to [C,D]
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+category"} interval
            = succeed "-category." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SS
                    %mi n
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SS n Cat
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to [2,D]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [-,-]
            means something different in the two cases.) Now inspection of how this would work reveals that the relationship of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_{g f}
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi g
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_g
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_f
            in the first picture corresponds to the
            %em functoriality
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to [2,D]
            in the second picture. That is, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mi f
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi g
                  %mo .
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_{g f} = \alpha_g . \alpha_f
            (and so on) then the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to [2,D]
            is a strict functor, while if it is only equivalent, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to [2,D]
            will be only a weak functor. Hence, if we allow only strict functors, then all our transformations must be “semistrict” in the sense that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi g
                    %mo .
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_{g f} = \alpha_g . \alpha_f
          %p
            Now, however, we meet the second horn of the dilemma: in an
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: F \to G
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %mo :
                  %mi G
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta: G\to H
            are semistrict transformations. Then we can define the components of a transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi β
                  %mo .
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \beta.\alpha:F\to H
            in the obvious way, but is it semistrict? If you try to prove that it is, you’ll find that you need a strict interchange law. (There is a pentagon involving all the ways to compose a 2x2 grid of square 2-cells, a picture of which can be found in Crans’ paper cited below.) So we are stuck.
          %p
            What can we do? Well, for one thing, the theorem is only about
            %em closed
            structures, not about
            %em monoidal
            structures. And Sjoerd Crans did manage to construct a non-closed “Gray-like” monoidal structure on Gray-cat. But it’s not clear whether the resulting notion of “Gray-cat-category” is in fact semistrict (i.e. whether arbitrary 4-categories could be strictified to them).
          %p
            Another way to escape would be to allow weak functors. This is not much good all by itself, since algebraic
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+definition+of+higher+category"} geometric definition of higher category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Verity-Gray+tensor+product"} Verity-Gray tensor product
            of weak complicial sets). This is one reason why non-algebraic notions of higher category are very attractive.
          %p
            Finally, a third escape that suggests itself would be to look for a notion of algebraic semistrict
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
